(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("cloud-iam",{"cloud-iam/identities-principals-and-permissions":{"html":"\u003cp>Every cloud action is a request: someone or something asks to do an action on a resource. The cloud checks identity, then permissions, then conditions, and answers allow or deny. Almost every security problem in the cloud is a mistake in that chain: the wrong identity, too many permissions, or a condition nobody checked. This lesson gives you the vocabulary before the provider-specific lessons.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Separate authentication from authorization\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Name the parts of an access decision\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tell human identities from workload identities\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spot the most common identity mistakes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"authentication-and-authorization\">Authentication and authorization\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Authentication\u003c/strong> answers &quot;who are you?&quot; with a password, a hardware key, a token or a certificate. \u003cstrong>Authorization\u003c/strong> answers &quot;what may you do?&quot; by comparing the request with policies. A system can authenticate you correctly and still wrongly authorize you, and the second mistake is the common one. Multi-factor authentication protects the first step; least privilege protects the second.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-parts-of-a-decision\">The parts of a decision\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Think of four fields. The \u003cstrong>principal\u003c/strong> is who asks (a person, a service, a pipeline). The \u003cstrong>action\u003c/strong> is what they want (read an object, start a server). The \u003cstrong>resource\u003c/strong> is the thing acted on (one bucket, one subscription). The \u003cstrong>conditions\u003c/strong> narrow it further (only from this network, only today, only with MFA). A policy is a statement that allows or denies a combination of these. Most clouds start from deny by default: with no matching allow, the answer is no. An explicit deny beats any allow.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"humans-and-workloads\">Humans and workloads\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>People sign in through a central identity provider, ideally with single sign-on and MFA, and receive short-lived sessions. Workloads such as a virtual machine, a function or a Kubernetes pod need identities too. The safe pattern is an identity that the platform attaches to the workload, which gives it temporary credentials automatically. The unsafe pattern is a long-lived access key copied into a configuration file or a repository, where it is leaked, shared and never rotated.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Using the root or owner account for daily work; creating one shared login for a team; giving administrator rights &quot;just to get it working&quot;; leaving keys with no expiry; and never removing access when someone changes team. Treat identities like inventory: know who has what, why, and when it ends.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A pipeline needs to upload files to one storage bucket. Which identity is the best choice?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A dedicated workload identity with short-lived credentials and permission only to write to that one bucket.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Specific principal, specific action, specific resource.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-key-in-the-repository\">Incident: The key in the repository\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A test key for a deploy pipeline was committed to a repository and found by a scanner three days later.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git log --oneline -1 -- deploy/.env\naws iam list-access-keys --user-name ci-deploy\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline -1 -- deploy/.env\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">3f9a1c2 add deploy env\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam list-access-keys --user-name ci-deploy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">AccessKeyId: AKIA...EXAMPLE  Status: Active  CreateDate: 2024-03-02\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The key belonged to a user with broad permissions and no expiry. The team deactivated it, reviewed the audit log for use from unknown addresses, replaced it with a role that the pipeline assumes for short sessions, and added secret scanning before every commit lands.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes-2\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using the root account for daily work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sharing one login between people\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Long-lived keys in code or files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping MFA for administrators\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"authentication-and-authorization","text":"Authentication and authorization"},{"id":"the-parts-of-a-decision","text":"The parts of a decision"},{"id":"humans-and-workloads","text":"Humans and workloads"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"},{"id":"incident-the-key-in-the-repository","text":"Incident: The key in the repository"},{"id":"common-mistakes-2","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS IAM user guide","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/introduction.html"},{"t":"Azure RBAC overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/overview"}],"text":"Every cloud action is a request: someone or something asks to do an action on a resource. The cloud checks identity, then permissions, then conditions, and answers allow or deny. Almost every security problem in the cloud is a mistake in that chain: the wrong identity, too many permissions, or a condition nobody checked. This lesson gives you the vocabulary before the provider-specific lessons. What you will learn Separate authentication from authorization Name the parts of an access decision Tell human identities from workload identities Spot the most common identity mistakes Authentication and authorization Authentication answers \"who are you?\" with a password, a hardware key, a token or a certificate. Authorization answers \"what may you do?\" by comparing the request with policies. A system can authenticate you correctly and still wrongly authorize you, and the second mistake is the common one. Multi-factor authentication protects the first step; least privilege protects the second. The parts of a decision Think of four fields. The principal is who asks (a person, a service, a pipeline). The action is what they want (read an object, start a server). The resource is the thing acted on (one bucket, one subscription). The conditions narrow it further (only from this network, only today, only with MFA). A policy is a statement that allows or denies a combination of these. Most clouds start from deny by default: with no matching allow, the answer is no. An explicit deny beats any allow. Humans and workloads People sign in through a central identity provider, ideally with single sign-on and MFA, and receive short-lived sessions. Workloads such as a virtual machine, a function or a Kubernetes pod need identities too. The safe pattern is an identity that the platform attaches to the workload, which gives it temporary credentials automatically. The unsafe pattern is a long-lived access key copied into a configuration file or a repository, where it is leaked, shared and never rotated. Common mistakes Using the root or owner account for daily work; creating one shared login for a team; giving administrator rights \"just to get it working\"; leaving keys with no expiry; and never removing access when someone changes team. Treat identities like inventory: know who has what, why, and when it ends. Predict first. A pipeline needs to upload files to one storage bucket. Which identity is the best choice? Your guess Answer: A dedicated workload identity with short-lived credentials and permission only to write to that one bucket. Specific principal, specific action, specific resource. Incident: The key in the repository Incident A test key for a deploy pipeline was committed to a repository and found by a scanner three days later. $ git log --oneline -1 -- deploy/.env 3f9a1c2 add deploy env $ aws iam list-access-keys --user-name ci-deploy AccessKeyId: AKIA...EXAMPLE Status: Active CreateDate: 2024-03-02 The key belonged to a user with broad permissions and no expiry. The team deactivated it, reviewed the audit log for use from unknown addresses, replaced it with a role that the pipeline assumes for short sessions, and added secret scanning before every commit lands. Common mistakes Using the root account for daily work Sharing one login between people Long-lived keys in code or files Skipping MFA for administrators"},"cloud-iam/aws-iam-policies-roles-and-assume-role":{"html":"\u003cp>AWS Identity and Access Management is the permission system for the whole account. You will meet users, groups, roles and policies. The most important idea is the role: an identity with no permanent password or key that a trusted principal can assume for a short session. If you remember one rule, make it this one: people and workloads should assume roles, not hold long-lived keys.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Read a JSON policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Know the difference between identity-based and resource-based policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use roles and assume-role for workloads and cross-account access\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Understand how boundaries and service control policies limit permissions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-a-policy\">Reading a policy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A policy lists statements. Each has an effect (Allow or Deny), actions, resources and optional conditions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{\n  &quot;Version&quot;: &quot;2012-10-17&quot;,\n  &quot;Statement&quot;: [{\n    &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;,\n    &quot;Action&quot;: [&quot;s3:GetObject&quot;],\n    &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:s3:::reports-prod/summaries/*&quot;\n  }]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Version&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2012-10-17&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Statement&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Effect&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Allow&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Action&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;s3:GetObject&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;Resource&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;arn:aws:s3:::reports-prod/summaries/*&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This allows reading objects under one prefix of one bucket and nothing else. A wildcard such as \u003ccode>&quot;Action&quot;: &quot;*&quot;\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>&quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;\u003c/code> is administrator access.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-kinds-of-policy\">Two kinds of policy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>identity-based policy\u003c/strong> is attached to a user, group or role and says what that identity may do. A \u003cstrong>resource-based policy\u003c/strong> is attached to a resource, such as a bucket or a queue, and says who may use it, including principals from other accounts. A request is allowed when the right policies allow it and nothing denies it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"roles-and-assume-role\">Roles and assume-role\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A role has two parts: a \u003cstrong>trust policy\u003c/strong> saying who may assume it, and \u003cstrong>permission policies\u003c/strong> saying what it can do. A caller asks the Security Token Service to assume the role and receives temporary credentials, usually valid for an hour or less. Compute services attach roles directly: an instance profile for a virtual machine, an execution role for a function, a task role for a container. Cross-account access works the same way: the target account trusts a role in the source account.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits-above-policies\">Limits above policies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>permissions boundary\u003c/strong> caps what an identity can ever be granted. A \u003cstrong>service control policy\u003c/strong> in AWS Organizations caps what whole accounts can do, even for their administrators. They never grant by themselves; they only restrict. Use them for guardrails such as &quot;no one may disable audit logging&quot; or &quot;only approved regions&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A role has an allow for s3:* but an organization policy denies deleting objects. Can the role delete?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> No, because the organization policy limits the account and an explicit restriction wins.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Guardrails restrict what policies can allow.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-wildcard-that-opened-the-bucket\">Incident: The wildcard that opened the bucket\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A developer fixed an access error by granting s3:* on all buckets to a build role.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws iam get-role-policy --role-name build --policy-name tmp\naws iam simulate-principal-policy --policy-source-arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/build --action-names s3:DeleteObject --resource-arns arn:aws:s3:::customer-exports/a.csv\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam get-role-policy --role-name build --policy-name tmp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&quot;Action&quot;: &quot;s3:*&quot;, &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam simulate-principal-policy --policy-source-arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/build --action-names s3:DeleteObject --resource-arns arn:aws:s3:::customer-exports/a.csv\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">EvalDecision: allowed\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The simulator showed the build role could delete customer exports. The team replaced the wildcard with the one bucket and prefix the build needs, added a boundary that denies deletes on data buckets, and put policy checks into code review.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Granting &quot;*&quot; to make an error disappear\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using IAM users with keys for workloads\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trust policies that allow any principal\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting resource-based policies when debugging\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"reading-a-policy","text":"Reading a policy"},{"id":"two-kinds-of-policy","text":"Two kinds of policy"},{"id":"roles-and-assume-role","text":"Roles and assume-role"},{"id":"limits-above-policies","text":"Limits above policies"},{"id":"incident-the-wildcard-that-opened-the-bucket","text":"Incident: The wildcard that opened the bucket"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"IAM policy evaluation logic","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_evaluation-logic.html"},{"t":"AWS IAM user guide","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/introduction.html"}],"text":"AWS Identity and Access Management is the permission system for the whole account. You will meet users, groups, roles and policies. The most important idea is the role: an identity with no permanent password or key that a trusted principal can assume for a short session. If you remember one rule, make it this one: people and workloads should assume roles, not hold long-lived keys. What you will learn Read a JSON policy Know the difference between identity-based and resource-based policies Use roles and assume-role for workloads and cross-account access Understand how boundaries and service control policies limit permissions Reading a policy A policy lists statements. Each has an effect (Allow or Deny), actions, resources and optional conditions. { \"Version\" : \"2012-10-17\" , \"Statement\" : [ { \"Effect\" : \"Allow\" , \"Action\" : [ \"s3:GetObject\" ] , \"Resource\" : \"arn:aws:s3:::reports-prod/summaries/*\" } ] } This allows reading objects under one prefix of one bucket and nothing else. A wildcard such as \"Action\": \"*\" with \"Resource\": \"*\" is administrator access. Two kinds of policy An identity-based policy is attached to a user, group or role and says what that identity may do. A resource-based policy is attached to a resource, such as a bucket or a queue, and says who may use it, including principals from other accounts. A request is allowed when the right policies allow it and nothing denies it. Roles and assume-role A role has two parts: a trust policy saying who may assume it, and permission policies saying what it can do. A caller asks the Security Token Service to assume the role and receives temporary credentials, usually valid for an hour or less. Compute services attach roles directly: an instance profile for a virtual machine, an execution role for a function, a task role for a container. Cross-account access works the same way: the target account trusts a role in the source account. Limits above policies A permissions boundary caps what an identity can ever be granted. A service control policy in AWS Organizations caps what whole accounts can do, even for their administrators. They never grant by themselves; they only restrict. Use them for guardrails such as \"no one may disable audit logging\" or \"only approved regions\". Predict first. A role has an allow for s3:* but an organization policy denies deleting objects. Can the role delete? Your guess Answer: No, because the organization policy limits the account and an explicit restriction wins. Guardrails restrict what policies can allow. Incident: The wildcard that opened the bucket Incident A developer fixed an access error by granting s3:* on all buckets to a build role. $ aws iam get-role-policy --role-name build --policy-name tmp \"Action\": \"s3:*\", \"Resource\": \"*\" $ aws iam simulate-principal-policy --policy-source-arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/build --action-names s3:DeleteObject --resource-arns arn:aws:s3:::customer-exports/a.csv EvalDecision: allowed The simulator showed the build role could delete customer exports. The team replaced the wildcard with the one bucket and prefix the build needs, added a boundary that denies deletes on data buckets, and put policy checks into code review. Common mistakes Granting \"*\" to make an error disappear Using IAM users with keys for workloads Trust policies that allow any principal Forgetting resource-based policies when debugging"},"cloud-iam/azure-entra-id-rbac-and-managed-identities":{"html":"\u003cp>Azure splits identity from access to resources. Microsoft Entra ID is the directory that holds users, groups and applications. Azure role-based access control decides what those identities can do to subscriptions, resource groups and resources. Learning to keep the two ideas apart makes most Azure access questions easy to answer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Explain Entra ID and Azure RBAC\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose the right scope for a role assignment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use managed identities instead of secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use conditional access and privileged access sensibly\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"directory-and-resources\">Directory and resources\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Entra ID authenticates people and applications and issues tokens. It also has directory roles, such as managing users, which are separate from Azure resource roles. Azure RBAC governs resources: a \u003cstrong>role definition\u003c/strong> lists allowed actions, a \u003cstrong>scope\u003c/strong> says where it applies, and a \u003cstrong>role assignment\u003c/strong> ties a principal to a role at a scope.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scopes-and-inheritance\">Scopes and inheritance\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Scopes form a hierarchy: management group, subscription, resource group, resource. An assignment at a higher scope is inherited by everything below it. Assign roles at the lowest scope that works. Giving Contributor on a subscription to fix one resource group problem grants access to everything in it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"built-in-roles\">Built-in roles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with built-in roles such as Reader, Contributor and Owner, and narrower ones like Storage Blob Data Reader. Owner can also assign roles, so reserve it. Note that control-plane roles and data-plane roles differ: being Contributor on a storage account does not automatically allow reading blob data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"managed-identities\">Managed identities\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>managed identity\u003c/strong> is an identity that Azure creates for a resource, such as a virtual machine, app service or function, and manages for you. The application requests a token from a local endpoint and uses it to reach other services, with no secret to store or rotate. A system-assigned identity lives and dies with its resource; a user-assigned identity is created separately and can be shared.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protecting-sign-ins\">Protecting sign-ins\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Conditional access policies require MFA, compliant devices or trusted locations. Privileged Identity Management gives administrators just-in-time, time-limited elevation with approval and logs, instead of standing permanent rights.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> An app needs to read secrets from a key vault. Which pattern avoids storing a credential?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Give the app a managed identity and assign it a narrow role on that key vault.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The platform supplies the token.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-contributor-at-the-subscription\">Incident: Contributor at the subscription\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A contractor needed to restart one web app and was made Contributor on the whole subscription.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"az role assignment list --assignee contractor@example.com --all -o table\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>az role assignment list --assignee contractor@example.com --all -o table\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Role         Scope\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Contributor  /subscriptions/0000-1111\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The contractor could change every resource, including production databases. The team removed the assignment, created a custom role allowing only restart on that app, assigned it at the resource scope, and enabled time-limited elevation for similar needs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assigning roles at the subscription level by default\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Confusing directory roles with resource roles\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing client secrets when a managed identity works\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving permanent Owner rights\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"directory-and-resources","text":"Directory and resources"},{"id":"scopes-and-inheritance","text":"Scopes and inheritance"},{"id":"built-in-roles","text":"Built-in roles"},{"id":"managed-identities","text":"Managed identities"},{"id":"protecting-sign-ins","text":"Protecting sign-ins"},{"id":"incident-contributor-at-the-subscription","text":"Incident: Contributor at the subscription"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Azure RBAC overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/overview"},{"t":"Managed identities for Azure resources","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/managed-identities-azure-resources/overview"}],"text":"Azure splits identity from access to resources. Microsoft Entra ID is the directory that holds users, groups and applications. Azure role-based access control decides what those identities can do to subscriptions, resource groups and resources. Learning to keep the two ideas apart makes most Azure access questions easy to answer. What you will learn Explain Entra ID and Azure RBAC Choose the right scope for a role assignment Use managed identities instead of secrets Use conditional access and privileged access sensibly Directory and resources Entra ID authenticates people and applications and issues tokens. It also has directory roles, such as managing users, which are separate from Azure resource roles. Azure RBAC governs resources: a role definition lists allowed actions, a scope says where it applies, and a role assignment ties a principal to a role at a scope. Scopes and inheritance Scopes form a hierarchy: management group, subscription, resource group, resource. An assignment at a higher scope is inherited by everything below it. Assign roles at the lowest scope that works. Giving Contributor on a subscription to fix one resource group problem grants access to everything in it. Built-in roles Start with built-in roles such as Reader, Contributor and Owner, and narrower ones like Storage Blob Data Reader. Owner can also assign roles, so reserve it. Note that control-plane roles and data-plane roles differ: being Contributor on a storage account does not automatically allow reading blob data. Managed identities A managed identity is an identity that Azure creates for a resource, such as a virtual machine, app service or function, and manages for you. The application requests a token from a local endpoint and uses it to reach other services, with no secret to store or rotate. A system-assigned identity lives and dies with its resource; a user-assigned identity is created separately and can be shared. Protecting sign-ins Conditional access policies require MFA, compliant devices or trusted locations. Privileged Identity Management gives administrators just-in-time, time-limited elevation with approval and logs, instead of standing permanent rights. Predict first. An app needs to read secrets from a key vault. Which pattern avoids storing a credential? Your guess Answer: Give the app a managed identity and assign it a narrow role on that key vault. The platform supplies the token. Incident: Contributor at the subscription Incident A contractor needed to restart one web app and was made Contributor on the whole subscription. $ az role assignment list --assignee contractor@example.com --all -o table Role Scope Contributor /subscriptions/0000-1111 The contractor could change every resource, including production databases. The team removed the assignment, created a custom role allowing only restart on that app, assigned it at the resource scope, and enabled time-limited elevation for similar needs. Common mistakes Assigning roles at the subscription level by default Confusing directory roles with resource roles Storing client secrets when a managed identity works Leaving permanent Owner rights"},"cloud-iam/least-privilege-and-access-reviews":{"html":"\u003cp>Least privilege means every identity has only the access its job needs, for only as long as needed. Nobody gets it perfect on day one, and permissions grow over time. The practical method is to start narrow, measure what is really used, remove the rest, and review on a schedule so access does not quietly accumulate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Describe least privilege in practice\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use access analysis tools to find unused permissions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Design roles around jobs, not people\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run a simple access review\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-narrow-then-measure\">Start narrow, then measure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Begin with the permissions you know are needed. When a request is denied, read the exact action and resource in the error and add only that. In the other direction, look at what existing identities actually use. AWS offers access analysis showing last-accessed services and can generate a policy from recorded activity; Azure and others show similar usage data. Remove what has not been used for a period such as 90 days, after checking with the owner.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"roles-for-jobs\">Roles for jobs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Create roles for functions (read-only auditor, deployer for one application, database operator) and assign people to them through groups. This avoids a unique set of permissions per person that nobody can review. Separate duties: the person who approves a change should not be the only one who can make and deploy it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"temporary-and-conditional-access\">Temporary and conditional access\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Prefer time-limited access for rare tasks, with approval and automatic expiry. Add conditions: require MFA, restrict to expected networks or regions, require a ticket reference in the session name. Break-glass accounts for emergencies should exist, be stored safely, be monitored, and trigger an alert when used.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"access-reviews\">Access reviews\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every quarter, list who has privileged access and ask the owner to confirm. Check service accounts, third-party integrations and old contractors. Record the date, the reviewer and the changes made. A review that finds nothing to remove for a year probably is not looking at real data.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A role has 400 permitted actions, but the logs show it used 12 in 90 days. What do you do?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Replace the policy with one that grants those 12 actions after confirming with the owner, and watch for denials.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Remove unused access.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-nobody-remembers-why\">Incident: Nobody remembers why\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A security review found 31 service accounts with administrator rights and no owner.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws iam generate-service-last-accessed-details --arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/legacy-batch\naws iam get-service-last-accessed-details --job-id 1234\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam generate-service-last-accessed-details --arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/legacy-batch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam get-service-last-accessed-details --job-id 1234\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ServiceNamespace: s3  LastAuthenticated: 2023-11-02\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ServiceNamespace: ec2  LastAuthenticated: -\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The report showed the role touched only one service, long ago. The team assigned owners, disabled unused accounts, replaced administrator rights with specific policies, and scheduled a quarterly review with a record of each decision.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Granting broad rights because tuning takes time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No owner for service accounts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reviews without usage data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No emergency access plan\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-narrow-then-measure","text":"Start narrow, then measure"},{"id":"roles-for-jobs","text":"Roles for jobs"},{"id":"temporary-and-conditional-access","text":"Temporary and conditional access"},{"id":"access-reviews","text":"Access reviews"},{"id":"incident-nobody-remembers-why","text":"Incident: Nobody remembers why"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Well-Architected: Security pillar","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/security-pillar/welcome.html"},{"t":"Azure RBAC overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/overview"}],"text":"Least privilege means every identity has only the access its job needs, for only as long as needed. Nobody gets it perfect on day one, and permissions grow over time. The practical method is to start narrow, measure what is really used, remove the rest, and review on a schedule so access does not quietly accumulate. What you will learn Describe least privilege in practice Use access analysis tools to find unused permissions Design roles around jobs, not people Run a simple access review Start narrow, then measure Begin with the permissions you know are needed. When a request is denied, read the exact action and resource in the error and add only that. In the other direction, look at what existing identities actually use. AWS offers access analysis showing last-accessed services and can generate a policy from recorded activity; Azure and others show similar usage data. Remove what has not been used for a period such as 90 days, after checking with the owner. Roles for jobs Create roles for functions (read-only auditor, deployer for one application, database operator) and assign people to them through groups. This avoids a unique set of permissions per person that nobody can review. Separate duties: the person who approves a change should not be the only one who can make and deploy it. Temporary and conditional access Prefer time-limited access for rare tasks, with approval and automatic expiry. Add conditions: require MFA, restrict to expected networks or regions, require a ticket reference in the session name. Break-glass accounts for emergencies should exist, be stored safely, be monitored, and trigger an alert when used. Access reviews Every quarter, list who has privileged access and ask the owner to confirm. Check service accounts, third-party integrations and old contractors. Record the date, the reviewer and the changes made. A review that finds nothing to remove for a year probably is not looking at real data. Predict first. A role has 400 permitted actions, but the logs show it used 12 in 90 days. What do you do? Your guess Answer: Replace the policy with one that grants those 12 actions after confirming with the owner, and watch for denials. Remove unused access. Incident: Nobody remembers why Incident A security review found 31 service accounts with administrator rights and no owner. $ aws iam generate-service-last-accessed-details --arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/legacy-batch $ aws iam get-service-last-accessed-details --job-id 1234 ServiceNamespace: s3 LastAuthenticated: 2023-11-02 ServiceNamespace: ec2 LastAuthenticated: - The report showed the role touched only one service, long ago. The team assigned owners, disabled unused accounts, replaced administrator rights with specific policies, and scheduled a quarterly review with a record of each decision. Common mistakes Granting broad rights because tuning takes time No owner for service accounts Reviews without usage data No emergency access plan"},"cloud-iam/secrets-and-encryption-with-kms-and-key-vaults":{"html":"\u003cp>Passwords, tokens and keys must live somewhere. The wrong places are source code, container images, chat messages and shared documents. The right place is a managed secrets store, with access controlled by identity, every read logged, and rotation built in. Encryption keys are a related but different subject, and cloud key management services keep them safe so you never handle raw key material.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choose a place for secrets and keep them out of code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain encryption at rest and in transit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Understand keys, envelope encryption and key policies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rotate secrets safely\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets-stores\">Secrets stores\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>AWS Secrets Manager, AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store and Azure Key Vault store secrets and return them to authorised identities at run time. The application fetches the value with its workload identity, holds it in memory and never writes it to disk or logs. Access is a permission like any other, so a pipeline may read only its own secrets. Every read appears in the audit log.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keys-and-envelope-encryption\">Keys and envelope encryption\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A key management service (AWS KMS, Azure Key Vault keys, Google Cloud KMS) creates keys and performs encryption without exposing the key. For large data, \u003cstrong>envelope encryption\u003c/strong> is used: a data key encrypts the data, and the master key in KMS encrypts the data key. Storing the encrypted data key beside the data keeps performance high and control central. Key policies decide who can use and who can manage a key; separate those two powers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"at-rest-and-in-transit\">At rest and in transit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Encrypt storage at rest by default for disks, databases and object storage, using managed or customer-managed keys when rules demand control. Encrypt traffic in transit with TLS and reject plain connections. Encryption does not replace access control: anyone with permission to decrypt sees the plain data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rotation\">Rotation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Rotate credentials on a schedule and immediately after exposure. Safe rotation supports two valid versions for a while: create the new, switch consumers, then retire the old. Managed rotation for database passwords can do this automatically. Prefer short-lived tokens, which make rotation a non-event.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A database password is needed by a service. Where should it live and how should the service get it?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> In a secrets manager; the service fetches it at start-up using its workload identity.\u003c/p>\u003cp>No password in code or images.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-environment-variable-in-the-image\">Incident: The environment variable in the image\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A container image published to a registry contained a database password in an environment variable.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker history --no-trunc shop-api:2.3 | grep DB_PASSWORD\ntrivy image --scanners secret shop-api:2.3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> --no-trunc shop-api:2.3 | grep DB_PASSWORD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ENV DB_PASSWORD=Sup3rS3cret!\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>trivy image --scanners secret shop-api:2.3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HIGH: secret in layer 4\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Anyone who could pull the image could read the password. The team rotated the password, rebuilt the image without it, moved the value to the secrets manager with access limited to the service role, and added secret scanning to the build.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Secrets in code, images or logs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One key for everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No rotation plan\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving everyone permission to decrypt\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"secrets-stores","text":"Secrets stores"},{"id":"keys-and-envelope-encryption","text":"Keys and envelope encryption"},{"id":"at-rest-and-in-transit","text":"At rest and in transit"},{"id":"rotation","text":"Rotation"},{"id":"incident-the-environment-variable-in-the-image","text":"Incident: The environment variable in the image"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Well-Architected: Security pillar","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/security-pillar/welcome.html"},{"t":"Managed identities for Azure resources","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/managed-identities-azure-resources/overview"}],"text":"Passwords, tokens and keys must live somewhere. The wrong places are source code, container images, chat messages and shared documents. The right place is a managed secrets store, with access controlled by identity, every read logged, and rotation built in. Encryption keys are a related but different subject, and cloud key management services keep them safe so you never handle raw key material. What you will learn Choose a place for secrets and keep them out of code Explain encryption at rest and in transit Understand keys, envelope encryption and key policies Rotate secrets safely Secrets stores AWS Secrets Manager, AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store and Azure Key Vault store secrets and return them to authorised identities at run time. The application fetches the value with its workload identity, holds it in memory and never writes it to disk or logs. Access is a permission like any other, so a pipeline may read only its own secrets. Every read appears in the audit log. Keys and envelope encryption A key management service (AWS KMS, Azure Key Vault keys, Google Cloud KMS) creates keys and performs encryption without exposing the key. For large data, envelope encryption is used: a data key encrypts the data, and the master key in KMS encrypts the data key. Storing the encrypted data key beside the data keeps performance high and control central. Key policies decide who can use and who can manage a key; separate those two powers. At rest and in transit Encrypt storage at rest by default for disks, databases and object storage, using managed or customer-managed keys when rules demand control. Encrypt traffic in transit with TLS and reject plain connections. Encryption does not replace access control: anyone with permission to decrypt sees the plain data. Rotation Rotate credentials on a schedule and immediately after exposure. Safe rotation supports two valid versions for a while: create the new, switch consumers, then retire the old. Managed rotation for database passwords can do this automatically. Prefer short-lived tokens, which make rotation a non-event. Predict first. A database password is needed by a service. Where should it live and how should the service get it? Your guess Answer: In a secrets manager; the service fetches it at start-up using its workload identity. No password in code or images. Incident: The environment variable in the image Incident A container image published to a registry contained a database password in an environment variable. $ docker history --no-trunc shop-api:2.3 | grep DB_PASSWORD ENV DB_PASSWORD=Sup3rS3cret! $ trivy image --scanners secret shop-api:2.3 HIGH: secret in layer 4 Anyone who could pull the image could read the password. The team rotated the password, rebuilt the image without it, moved the value to the secrets manager with access limited to the service role, and added secret scanning to the build. Common mistakes Secrets in code, images or logs One key for everything No rotation plan Giving everyone permission to decrypt"},"cloud-iam/network-boundaries-and-private-access":{"html":"\u003cp>Even perfect permissions are weaker when every resource is reachable from the internet. Network boundaries add a second layer: a service that cannot be reached cannot be attacked directly. Identity and network controls work together, and many cloud breaches began with a public storage bucket, an open database port or an unprotected management interface.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Use private subnets and security groups sensibly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reach cloud services privately with endpoints\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Close the common public exposures\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Combine network and identity controls\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"private-by-default\">Private by default\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Place application servers and databases in private subnets without public addresses. Put only load balancers and gateways in public subnets. Outbound traffic goes through a NAT gateway or a proxy you can monitor. In Azure, the same idea uses virtual networks, subnets and network security groups.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"security-groups-and-rules\">Security groups and rules\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A security group is a stateful firewall attached to a resource. Allow only the ports and sources needed, preferably referring to other security groups instead of wide address ranges. Be suspicious of \u003ccode>0.0.0.0/0\u003c/code> on anything other than a public web port, and never on SSH, RDP or database ports. Use session-based access (such as AWS Systems Manager Session Manager or Azure Bastion) instead of opening management ports.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"private-endpoints\">Private endpoints\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cloud services such as object storage, key vaults and databases are normally reached through public addresses. \u003cstrong>Private endpoints\u003c/strong> (AWS PrivateLink and VPC endpoints, Azure Private Link) give the service a private address inside your network, so traffic never crosses the internet. Combine them with policies that deny access from anywhere else.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"public-exposure-checklist\">Public exposure checklist\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Block public access on storage accounts and buckets at the account level; check for public snapshots and images; review public IP addresses regularly; protect web applications with a firewall and rate limits; and scan for open ports from outside. Remember that a network rule is not authorisation: a service that is reachable still has to check identity.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A database must be used only by the application servers. How do you configure it?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Keep it in a private subnet and allow its port only from the application security group.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Reference the group, not an address range.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-public-bucket\">Incident: The public bucket\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A storage bucket holding customer exports was reachable by anyone with the link.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws s3api get-public-access-block --bucket customer-exports\naws s3api get-bucket-policy-status --bucket customer-exports\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api get-public-access-block --bucket customer-exports\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">An error occurred (NoSuchPublicAccessBlockConfiguration)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3api get-bucket-policy-status --bucket customer-exports\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&quot;IsPublic&quot;: true\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The block on public access had never been enabled. The team turned it on for the whole account, replaced the sharing links with short-lived signed links, reviewed the access logs for outside reads, and added a rule that alerts on any public bucket.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Public IPs on databases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Wide source ranges on management ports\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming a private network replaces authentication\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No account-level block on public storage\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"private-by-default","text":"Private by default"},{"id":"security-groups-and-rules","text":"Security groups and rules"},{"id":"private-endpoints","text":"Private endpoints"},{"id":"public-exposure-checklist","text":"Public exposure checklist"},{"id":"incident-the-public-bucket","text":"Incident: The public bucket"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Well-Architected: Security pillar","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/security-pillar/welcome.html"},{"t":"Azure RBAC overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/overview"}],"text":"Even perfect permissions are weaker when every resource is reachable from the internet. Network boundaries add a second layer: a service that cannot be reached cannot be attacked directly. Identity and network controls work together, and many cloud breaches began with a public storage bucket, an open database port or an unprotected management interface. What you will learn Use private subnets and security groups sensibly Reach cloud services privately with endpoints Close the common public exposures Combine network and identity controls Private by default Place application servers and databases in private subnets without public addresses. Put only load balancers and gateways in public subnets. Outbound traffic goes through a NAT gateway or a proxy you can monitor. In Azure, the same idea uses virtual networks, subnets and network security groups. Security groups and rules A security group is a stateful firewall attached to a resource. Allow only the ports and sources needed, preferably referring to other security groups instead of wide address ranges. Be suspicious of 0.0.0.0/0 on anything other than a public web port, and never on SSH, RDP or database ports. Use session-based access (such as AWS Systems Manager Session Manager or Azure Bastion) instead of opening management ports. Private endpoints Cloud services such as object storage, key vaults and databases are normally reached through public addresses. Private endpoints (AWS PrivateLink and VPC endpoints, Azure Private Link) give the service a private address inside your network, so traffic never crosses the internet. Combine them with policies that deny access from anywhere else. Public exposure checklist Block public access on storage accounts and buckets at the account level; check for public snapshots and images; review public IP addresses regularly; protect web applications with a firewall and rate limits; and scan for open ports from outside. Remember that a network rule is not authorisation: a service that is reachable still has to check identity. Predict first. A database must be used only by the application servers. How do you configure it? Your guess Answer: Keep it in a private subnet and allow its port only from the application security group. Reference the group, not an address range. Incident: The public bucket Incident A storage bucket holding customer exports was reachable by anyone with the link. $ aws s3api get-public-access-block --bucket customer-exports An error occurred (NoSuchPublicAccessBlockConfiguration) $ aws s3api get-bucket-policy-status --bucket customer-exports \"IsPublic\": true The block on public access had never been enabled. The team turned it on for the whole account, replaced the sharing links with short-lived signed links, reviewed the access logs for outside reads, and added a rule that alerts on any public bucket. Common mistakes Public IPs on databases Wide source ranges on management ports Assuming a private network replaces authentication No account-level block on public storage"},"cloud-iam/logging-detection-and-audit-trails":{"html":"\u003cp>You cannot investigate what you did not record. Cloud providers log administrative actions: AWS CloudTrail, the Azure Activity Log, and Google Cloud Audit Logs. These logs tell you who did what, when, from where. Collected, protected and watched, they turn a breach from an unknown loss into a time-ordered story.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Enable organisation-wide audit logging\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Read an audit record\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Protect logs from tampering\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alert on high-risk actions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-log\">What to log\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Management events (create, change, delete), data events for sensitive storage, sign-in logs from the identity provider, network flow logs, and application logs. Turn on audit logging in every account and region from day one, ideally centrally for the whole organisation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-a-record\">Reading a record\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An audit record names the \u003cstrong>principal\u003c/strong>, the \u003cstrong>action\u003c/strong>, the \u003cstrong>resource\u003c/strong>, the \u003cstrong>source address\u003c/strong>, the \u003cstrong>time\u003c/strong> and whether it succeeded.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{\n  &quot;eventTime&quot;: &quot;2024-05-14T02:11:09Z&quot;,\n  &quot;eventName&quot;: &quot;DeleteTrail&quot;,\n  &quot;userIdentity&quot;: { &quot;type&quot;: &quot;AssumedRole&quot;, &quot;arn&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/dev/anna&quot; },\n  &quot;sourceIPAddress&quot;: &quot;203.0.113.45&quot;,\n  &quot;errorCode&quot;: null\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;eventTime&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2024-05-14T02:11:09Z&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;eventName&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DeleteTrail&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;userIdentity&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;AssumedRole&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;arn&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/dev/anna&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;sourceIPAddress&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;203.0.113.45&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;errorCode&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">null\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Deleting a trail at 02:11 from an unfamiliar address is exactly the kind of event to alert on.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protect-the-logs\">Protect the logs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Send logs to a separate account that few people can use. Make the storage append-only or write-once, enable integrity validation, and deny deletion. An attacker who can erase the log can hide the attack.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"detect-and-respond\">Detect and respond\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with a short list of alerts: use of the root account, a change to logging, new access keys for privileged users, a policy that grants administrator rights, a public resource, sign-ins from unusual places, and many denied requests. Use managed threat detection services as a baseline. Write a short runbook for each alert: who looks, what they check, and when to disable the identity.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> An alert shows someone disabled audit logging at night. What do you do first?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Treat it as an incident: identify the principal, restore logging, review recent actions by that principal and disable the identity if it is not legitimate.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Logging changes are high risk.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-logging-was-off\">Incident: Logging was off\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During an investigation the team found a gap of nine days with no audit records.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudtrail get-trail-status --name org-trail\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail get-trail-status --name org-trail\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">IsLogging: false\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">LatestDeliveryTime: 2024-05-05T01:02:13Z\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A cost-cutting change had stopped the trail. The team re-enabled it, added an organisation policy preventing anyone from stopping it, set an alert on the stop event, and sent copies to a locked-down log account.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Logging only in some regions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logs in the same account anyone can edit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert on logging changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Collecting logs nobody reads\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-to-log","text":"What to log"},{"id":"reading-a-record","text":"Reading a record"},{"id":"protect-the-logs","text":"Protect the logs"},{"id":"detect-and-respond","text":"Detect and respond"},{"id":"incident-logging-was-off","text":"Incident: Logging was off"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Well-Architected: Security pillar","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/security-pillar/welcome.html"},{"t":"Azure RBAC overview","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/role-based-access-control/overview"}],"text":"You cannot investigate what you did not record. Cloud providers log administrative actions: AWS CloudTrail, the Azure Activity Log, and Google Cloud Audit Logs. These logs tell you who did what, when, from where. Collected, protected and watched, they turn a breach from an unknown loss into a time-ordered story. What you will learn Enable organisation-wide audit logging Read an audit record Protect logs from tampering Alert on high-risk actions What to log Management events (create, change, delete), data events for sensitive storage, sign-in logs from the identity provider, network flow logs, and application logs. Turn on audit logging in every account and region from day one, ideally centrally for the whole organisation. Reading a record An audit record names the principal , the action , the resource , the source address , the time and whether it succeeded. { \"eventTime\" : \"2024-05-14T02:11:09Z\" , \"eventName\" : \"DeleteTrail\" , \"userIdentity\" : { \"type\" : \"AssumedRole\" , \"arn\" : \"arn:aws:sts::123456789012:assumed-role/dev/anna\" } , \"sourceIPAddress\" : \"203.0.113.45\" , \"errorCode\" : null } Deleting a trail at 02:11 from an unfamiliar address is exactly the kind of event to alert on. Protect the logs Send logs to a separate account that few people can use. Make the storage append-only or write-once, enable integrity validation, and deny deletion. An attacker who can erase the log can hide the attack. Detect and respond Start with a short list of alerts: use of the root account, a change to logging, new access keys for privileged users, a policy that grants administrator rights, a public resource, sign-ins from unusual places, and many denied requests. Use managed threat detection services as a baseline. Write a short runbook for each alert: who looks, what they check, and when to disable the identity. Predict first. An alert shows someone disabled audit logging at night. What do you do first? Your guess Answer: Treat it as an incident: identify the principal, restore logging, review recent actions by that principal and disable the identity if it is not legitimate. Logging changes are high risk. Incident: Logging was off Incident During an investigation the team found a gap of nine days with no audit records. $ aws cloudtrail get-trail-status --name org-trail IsLogging: false LatestDeliveryTime: 2024-05-05T01:02:13Z A cost-cutting change had stopped the trail. The team re-enabled it, added an organisation policy preventing anyone from stopping it, set an alert on the stop event, and sent copies to a locked-down log account. Common mistakes Logging only in some regions Logs in the same account anyone can edit No alert on logging changes Collecting logs nobody reads"},"cloud-iam/debugging-access-denied-and-cloud-security-incidents":{"html":"\u003cp>Two situations test everything in this skill. In the first, a legitimate request fails with &quot;access denied&quot; and you need the narrowest fix. In the second, a credential has leaked and you need to contain damage quickly. Both reward a calm, ordered method.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Read an access-denied error\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Walk the evaluation chain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose a narrow fix\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Respond to a leaked credential in order\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"read-the-error\">Read the error\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A good error names the principal, the action and the resource: &quot;User arn:...:role/build is not authorized to perform s3:PutObject on arn:aws:s3:::reports-prod/x&quot;. That is already the answer to three questions. Check that the principal is the one you expect, because many failures are caused by running as a different role than intended.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"walk-the-chain\">Walk the chain\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Check in order: is the right identity in use; does an identity policy allow the action on this resource; does a resource policy allow or deny it; does a boundary, organisation policy or deny statement restrict it; do conditions (network, MFA, tags, time) hold; and for data in storage or keys, does the key policy allow use. Policy simulators and the access analysis tools show which statement decided. Audit logs show the denied call with its error.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fix-narrowly\">Fix narrowly\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Add the specific action and resource, not a wildcard. If the cause is a missing condition such as the request not coming through the private endpoint, fix the path rather than removing the condition. Record why the access is needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"suspected-credential-leak\">Suspected credential leak\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Act in this order: \u003cstrong>contain\u003c/strong> (deactivate the key or revoke the session, do not delete it yet); \u003cstrong>assess\u003c/strong> (audit logs for use of that credential, new users or keys, new resources, changed policies, copies of data); \u003cstrong>eradicate\u003c/strong> (remove attacker-created access, rotate related secrets); \u003cstrong>recover\u003c/strong> (replace with a role or short-lived credential); \u003cstrong>learn\u003c/strong> (how did it leak, what detection would have caught it sooner). Tell the right people early.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A leaked key shows calls from an unknown address creating servers. What is your first action?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Deactivate the key or revoke the session immediately, then review its activity and remove what it created.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Contain first.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-crypto-servers-in-six-regions\">Incident: Crypto servers in six regions\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A cloud bill spiked because someone launched large servers in regions the team never used.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIA...EXAMPLE --max-results 3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIA...EXAMPLE --max-results 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">RunInstances us-east-2  203.0.113.77\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">RunInstances ap-south-1 203.0.113.77\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CreateAccessKey  203.0.113.77\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The key had been exposed in a public repository. The team disabled it, terminated the servers, deleted the attacker-created key, checked for changed policies, enabled organisation guardrails that deny unused regions, and moved the application to roles.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Deleting evidence before capturing it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fixing access errors with wildcards\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rotating one secret while others stay exposed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No guardrail against unused regions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"read-the-error","text":"Read the error"},{"id":"walk-the-chain","text":"Walk the chain"},{"id":"fix-narrowly","text":"Fix narrowly"},{"id":"suspected-credential-leak","text":"Suspected credential leak"},{"id":"incident-crypto-servers-in-six-regions","text":"Incident: Crypto servers in six regions"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"IAM policy evaluation logic","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/reference_policies_evaluation-logic.html"},{"t":"AWS Well-Architected: Security pillar","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/security-pillar/welcome.html"}],"text":"Two situations test everything in this skill. In the first, a legitimate request fails with \"access denied\" and you need the narrowest fix. In the second, a credential has leaked and you need to contain damage quickly. Both reward a calm, ordered method. What you will learn Read an access-denied error Walk the evaluation chain Choose a narrow fix Respond to a leaked credential in order Read the error A good error names the principal, the action and the resource: \"User arn:...:role/build is not authorized to perform s3:PutObject on arn:aws:s3:::reports-prod/x\". That is already the answer to three questions. Check that the principal is the one you expect, because many failures are caused by running as a different role than intended. Walk the chain Check in order: is the right identity in use; does an identity policy allow the action on this resource; does a resource policy allow or deny it; does a boundary, organisation policy or deny statement restrict it; do conditions (network, MFA, tags, time) hold; and for data in storage or keys, does the key policy allow use. Policy simulators and the access analysis tools show which statement decided. Audit logs show the denied call with its error. Fix narrowly Add the specific action and resource, not a wildcard. If the cause is a missing condition such as the request not coming through the private endpoint, fix the path rather than removing the condition. Record why the access is needed. Suspected credential leak Act in this order: contain (deactivate the key or revoke the session, do not delete it yet); assess (audit logs for use of that credential, new users or keys, new resources, changed policies, copies of data); eradicate (remove attacker-created access, rotate related secrets); recover (replace with a role or short-lived credential); learn (how did it leak, what detection would have caught it sooner). Tell the right people early. Predict first. A leaked key shows calls from an unknown address creating servers. What is your first action? Your guess Answer: Deactivate the key or revoke the session immediately, then review its activity and remove what it created. Contain first. Incident: Crypto servers in six regions Incident A cloud bill spiked because someone launched large servers in regions the team never used. $ aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIA...EXAMPLE --max-results 3 RunInstances us-east-2 203.0.113.77 RunInstances ap-south-1 203.0.113.77 CreateAccessKey 203.0.113.77 The key had been exposed in a public repository. The team disabled it, terminated the servers, deleted the attacker-created key, checked for changed policies, enabled organisation guardrails that deny unused regions, and moved the application to roles. Common mistakes Deleting evidence before capturing it Fixing access errors with wildcards Rotating one secret while others stay exposed No guardrail against unused regions"}});